</Routine>
</Routines>
</AddOnInstructionDefinition>
<AddOnInstructionDefinition Name="AxisConsumeCIPSync_AOI" Revision="1.4" Vendor="Rockwell Automation" ExecutePrescan="true" ExecutePostscan="false" ExecuteEnableInFalse="false" CreatedDate="2010-08-26T11:34:57.966Z" CreatedBy="EMEA\RGuenthe" EditedDate="2011-03-09T03:14:55.623Z" EditedBy="HIGROCKSOFT\Administrator" SoftwareRevision="v18.00"
>
<Description>
<![CDATA[Consume Axis via CIP Sync]]>
</Description>
<AdditionalHelpText>
<![CDATA[Add On Instruction AxisConsumeCIPSync_AOI

Function:
Add On Instructions AxisProduceCIPSync_xxx_AOI and AxisConsumeCIPSync_AOI provide a produced/consumed axis 
over different chassis utilizing CIP Sync time synchronization.

The AOIs must be used in an event task that reacts to motion group execution (Motion Event Task).

Errors Codes

0  No Fault
1  Producer CPU not in Run mode
2  Connection to producer faulted
3  Coarse Update producer is calculated as less or equal 0
4  Producer counter shows more than 20 scans since last update
5  Time difference Producer motion calculation  to Consumer motion calculation is larger than         CFG_TimeOut_ms time
6  No update from producer for  CFG_TimeOut_ms time
7  CFG_ExtrapolationType <0 or > 2
8  CFG_AdditionalDelayCompensation_us producer +  CFG_AdditionalDelayCompensation_us consumer > 5 x     Coarse Update Consumer
9  Consumed Axis (Virtual Axis connected to Out_AxisConsumed) has an error and can not be moved by          motion instructions
10 DisableAdvancedFeatures has been changed – requires a restart
11 DisableExtendedErrorCheck has been turned off  – requires a restart
12 System Time not synchronized for this CPU for longer than 1 minute
20 SERCOS Specific: MSG instruction to SERCOS Drive failed

Extended Error Codes

Extended errors are only evaluated if Cfg_DisableExtendedErrorCheck = 0 on the Produce AOI. 
If it is set to 1 none of the following errors will occur / are evaluated for.

13 Change in RotaryAxis configuration of the producer
14 Change in unwind configuration of the producer (Position Unwind or Conversion Constant changed)
15 Motion Group Autotag Update has been disabled
16 Axis instance has changed – happens if the axis is exchanged programmatically while online
17 AOI is not executed in a motion event task or AOI is executed several times within this task (may also 
    happen if backing tag is used several times for different AOI instances)
18 Producer changed from producing  Command to Actual or vice versa while producer is showing 
     ServoActionStatus = 1

Fault Behavior
Produce AOI Fault
If a produce AOI detects an error it sets Sts_ER, resets Sts_IP and stops filling the produce data structure 
in Out_AxisDataStructure.
As a result the Consume AOI will detect a timeout and by that also stop. Until the Consume AOI times out it 
will continue to run at the last set speed.

Consume AOI Fault
If the Consume AOI detects an error it will either
- ramp down with the given ramp in parameter Inp_StopRampOnFault_UnitsSec2
or
- continue to run at the last speed if  parameter Inp_StopRampOnFault_UnitsSec2 is 0
In both cases the Sts_ER bit will be set immediately.
Sts_IP will only go away if the AOI stops actively driving the consumed axis.
This happens when the end of the stop ramp is reached.

In case of a 0 stop ramp STS_IP stays set until the user stops the axis with an external motion instruction 
like an MAS (Motion Axis Stop).

Fault Recover
There are 2 ways to recover from a fault:

1) set Inp_Restart to 1
In this case the AOI will restart and start producing/ consuming after a few scans again.
This input reacts on positive edge only and has to be reset/set for the next restart.

2) set Inp_AutoRecover to 1
In this case the AOI will start to restart immediately after detecting a fault.
This input reacts to state. If set to 1 it will automatically start restart.

In any case the restart is not bumpless!
There will be a time of at least 6 scans where the consumed axis stops.

Controller Minor Faults

The AOIs will lead to Controller Minor Faults every 35 minutes. 
“An arithmetic overflow occurred in an instruction”, Type 4, Code 4
The reason is an integer operation that leads to sign overflows. To avoid this it would take a lot of more 
complex code, while it would not have any impact on the function.

If this behavior is not acceptable for any reason, please reset these minor faults after AOI execution.

For more information on Controller Faults review:
1756-pm014A-EN-P Logix5000Controllers Controller Major and Minor Faults

Features

-	Produce / Consume axes over EtherNet/IP
The feature works with the same synchronization accuracy as producing / consuming axes over the ControlLogix  backplane or with SynchLink modules to different chassis. (Note that unlike SynchLink CST is not synchronized between chassis.)

-	Support for all axis types / axis Technologies
Virtual, CIP Motion, SERCOS, Analog and “Feedback Only” axes connected to the drives auxiliary feedback

-	Support for Actual and Command position
Actual or Command position can be produced / consumed. For Feedback Only Axes the actual position has to be  selected. The consumed axis will react either to command position that is generated in the producing 
controller or the actual position which is the retrieved feedback position. 
If drives (AXIS_SERVO, AXIS_SERVO_DRIVE, AXIS_CIP) are turned off the producer will send only actual data. The reason is that the axis may move while ServoActionStatus=0 and the command values would not reflect that movement in velocity and acceleration, which is required for the consumer to move.

-	Support of all EtherNet/IP - CIP Sync topologies
Star, Linear with embedded switches or ETAP modules, Ring with ENxTR EtherNet/IP Modules

-	Stop Ramp on consumer after error detection e.g. Timeout from producer
The consumed axis can be ramped down in case of a timeout of producer data or other error reasons. If a stop  ramp is not desirable because the consumer should continue to run with last velocity the stop ramp can be set to 0. In this case the user program may detect the error and react with a later stop or any motion on the 
consumed (virtual) axis.

-	Automatic Delay Compensation 
For various reasons the produced and consumed axis have a time delay that needs to be compensated. Reasons are 
o	Actual Delay from physical position to actual position in the controller
o	Command Delay from controller command position to physical position 
o	Different Coarse Update Periods on producer and consumer
o	Data transfer delay between producer and consumer
The delay can also be negative. (Physical consumer reacts before producer) The delays are the same as with 
produced / consumed axes over SynchLink or the ControlLogix backplane.
All these delays are automatically compensated so all motion stays synchronous over the entire system.

-	Master Delay Compensation
All axes used in a Logix motion application have a feature called Master Delay Compensation. If axes are used  in Position Cam or in gearing this feature takes care of actual delays from drive to controller and command  delays from controller to drive, so the physical motor positions stay synchronous independent of the used drive technology. This master delay compensation is also used in the application code to keep all axes 
synchronized. Make sure that all axes used in a “gearing chain” have Master Delay compensation turned on!

-	Additional time compensation for other purposes
In some applications it is a desirable behavior that the consumed axis is delayed or delay compensated for a 
given time. Example: The consumed axis is the machine master that controls speed of the product. A geared 
axis controls an analog valve that applies glue to a moving product. The delay time for the glue to travel 
through air and arrive at the product is constant. This delay can be automatically compensated for by using 
the additional time compensation on the consumed master axis. As a result the glue will always hit the same 
spot on the product independent of the product/master speed. 

-	Selection of 1st or 2nd order Extrapolation for Delay Compensation
In most scenarios a 2nd order extrapolation provides accuracy advantaged during acceleration and 
deceleration. However, in applications with very noisy producers, like a feedback only axis with low 
resolution, a 1st order extrapolation can be selected.

-	CIP produced message failure or message delay extrapolation 
A message failure or a message that does not arrive in time will not affect the consumed axis.

-	Calculation of extrapolation position deviation from produced axis
With large time delays (that can be caused by high, different motion Coarse Update Periods), a position 
deviation between produced and consumer is introduced. This deviation can be calculated and displayed by the consuming AOI.

Advanced Features

For the majority of applications advanced features are not required but they have been implemented to cover 
mostly all applications that can utilize produced / consumed axes over Ethernet/IP. That includes high 
accuracy / low dynamics applications.

Advanced features can be turned off with CFG_DisableAdvancedFeatures = 1 on the producer AOI to minimize scan 
time on both the producer and the consumer in cases where advanced features do not apply.

-	Automatic MRP / Home detection of produced axis
The consumed axis will not execute motion to make up for the position difference but use an MRP at the same position

-	Dynamics Limit 
The extra-/interpolation algorithm introduces overshoots in acceleration and velocity compared to the 
produced axis. Enabling the dynamics limit will reduce the acceleration to a maximum of 2 x producer 
acceleration

-	Force extrapolation to move forward only (positive velocity) or backward only (negative velocity)
Extrapolation can overshoot and needs to turn backward afterwards. This parameter can be set to prevent the backward movement. Example: a film section should not turn backward at all to keep the tension on the film. In most situations this feature also prevents the algorithm from overshooting the producer stop position. 
However, this behavior is not explicitly programmed and tested and should not be relied on. 

-	Multiple unwind cycles per Coarse Update rate
A produced axis with cyclic unwind behavior can have a short unwind position. In case of large Coarse Update Periods the axis may travel for more than one unwind (more than one turn) per Coarse Update.
This feature will keep track of these unwind cycles.

-	Recover from Event Task overlap
The AOIs are used in an Event Task that is triggered by Motion Group Execution. If the scan time of this task 
is high compared to the Coarse Update Period it may overlap, which means the AOI is not executed for at least one Coarse Update Period. This feature takes care about this exceptional behavior.

-	32bit Float Quantization Error Compensation
The Logix Motion Engine works with 64bit floating point values. In application code there is a maximum length 
of 32bit floating point variables (real data type).
To allow high position values and low acceleration this feature has been implemented (Example: position 
20000Units, acceleration 100 Units/Sec2  would lead to a on/off pattern of acceleration due to quantization 
limits) 

-	CST Jitter Compensation Ramps
Coordinate System Time (CST) between different chassis is floating which means that there are time steps / 
jitter of 1 microsecond between the chassis. These time steps are of coarse very small compared to the 
required system accuracy. However, in some situations this will introduce very small glitches into the 
consumed axis dynamics. To avoid these glitches the CST jitter between producer and consumer is ramped in 
steps of 100 nanoseconds per Coarse Update.


Advanced features can be turned off with CFG_DisableAdvancedFeatures = 1 on the producer AOI to minimize scan time in cases where advanced features do not apply.
For the majority of applications advanced features are not required.
The user has to make sure that the advanced features are not required in his specific application.

Usage of InOut_Axis_CSTAid
This axis is only used internally within the AOIs.
If several AOIs are used per CPU the same virtual axes can be used.
The AOIs will handle it correctly even if several AOIs use the same axis.

See AOI parameters for detailed usage of parameters and AOI function.

Usage/situations that may lead to unexpected behavior
- use superimposed movements on consumed axis or Axis_CSTAid
- motion group overlap 
- Advanced feature required but CFG_DisableAdvancedFeatures = 1

Recommendations:
- Do not produce a consumed axis or an axis that is geared/cammed to a consumed axis. Always use the produced axis and consume it where required.
This is also true for axes used in the same chassis utilizing standard Logix axis produce/consume mechanisms. 
The extrapolation will introduce dynamics peaks to keep up with position when the producer axis starts. This 
effect will grow exponentially if you produce an axis that has been already consumed. Positions will be 
correct but high acceleration peaks are a result. 

- Use only switches with PTP transparent clocks between producer CPU and consumer CPU – this guarantees highest system synchronization accuracy







]]>
</AdditionalHelpText>
<Parameters>
<Parameter Name="EnableIn" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="false" ExternalAccess="Read Only">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Enable Input - System Defined Parameter]]>
</Description>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="EnableOut" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Output" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="false" ExternalAccess="Read Only">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Enable Output - System Defined Parameter]]>
</Description>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_AxisDataStructure" TagType="Base" DataType="UDT_CIPSyncProduceConsumeAxis" Usage="InOut" Required="true" Visible="true" Constant="false">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Consumed tag with data structure from producer]]>
</Description>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Out_Axis_Consumed" TagType="Base" DataType="AXIS_VIRTUAL" Usage="InOut" Required="true" Visible="true">
<Description>
<![CDATA["consumed" axis has to be configured as a virtual and will be controlled by this AOI]]>
</Description>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_MotionGroup" TagType="Base" DataType="MOTION_GROUP" Usage="InOut" Required="true" Visible="true">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Motion Group of the "consumed" axis]]>
</Description>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="InOut_Axis_CSTAid" TagType="Base" DataType="AXIS_VIRTUAL" Usage="InOut" Required="true" Visible="true">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Axis that is needed to determine timing for this CPU. The same axis can be used in several Produce/Consume AOIs on this CPU.]]>
</Description>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_Restart" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="true" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Reset / Restart - warning: with a restart it will take several scans until the consumed axis is moving again]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="BOOL" Radix="Decimal" Value="0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_AutoRecover" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Restart Automatically after an error occured - it will take several scans to recover.]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="BOOL" Radix="Decimal" Value="0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_Timeout_mSec" TagType="Base" DataType="DINT" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Timeout in milliseconds]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00 00 00 00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="DINT" Radix="Decimal" Value="0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_StopRampOnFault_UnitsSec2" TagType="Base" DataType="REAL" Usage="Input" Radix="Float" Required="false" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Stopramp of consumed axis if error occured in units/sec^2]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00 00 00 00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="REAL" Radix="Float" Value="0.0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_NoNegativeMovement" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[=1 prevents that extrapolation turns the consumed axis in negative direction]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="BOOL" Radix="Decimal" Value="0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_NoPositiveMovement" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[=1 prevents that extrapolation turns the consumed axis in positive direction]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="BOOL" Radix="Decimal" Value="0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_DynamicsLimit" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[=1 prevents that the consumed axis introduces high acceleration when the produced axis starts/stops. Acc is limited to 2 x producer acceleration. This will introduce a higher deviation between producer and consumer.]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="BOOL" Radix="Decimal" Value="0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
<Parameter Name="Inp_DisableAllDelayCompensations" TagType="Base" DataType="BOOL" Usage="Input" Radix="Decimal" Required="false" Visible="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[=1 alll delays in the system are discarded. The producer position is directly used to move the consumed axis. The  delays are not constant so this will create a changing deviation between producer and consumer but keep exactly the same dynamics.]]>
</Description>
<DefaultData>00</DefaultData>
<DefaultData Format="Decorated">
<DataValue DataType="BOOL" Radix="Decimal" Value="0"/>
</DefaultData>
</Parameter>
